  "findings": [
    "Run, keyed runners, keyed watch runners, and RunSweep establish the common telemetry owner and operation records. The exact main checks cover these runtime paths.",
    "Main delegates process ownership through Monitor. Process and queue ownership are separate from finite helper operations.",
    "Scan, ScanFrom, ScanStream, checkpointed scans, cycle scans, and RunObservation do not establish their own common controller telemetry owner or operation records. They retain the supplied context.",
    "Hypershell uses scans inside controller callbacks and cycle helpers for identity and account cleanup. Those calls can inherit the enclosing operation context, but that does not prove per-helper or per-action telemetry.",
    "Additional helper telemetry must preserve outer ownership, callback contexts, retry semantics, checkpoint commits, and failure priority. Standalone helper tests need explicit coverage before a broad C6 claim."
  ],
